    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/2 disable\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/1 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" is to remove the \"disable\" statement from the interface configuration. The \"delete\" command is used to remove configuration statements in Juniper. I\\'ve included all three interface commands since they all had \"no shutdown\" in the original configuration.', 'trans_template': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/2 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/2 disable\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/1 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" is to remove the \"disable\" statement from the interface configuration. The \"delete\" command is used to remove configuration statements in Juniper. I\\'ve included all three interface commands since they all had \"no shutdown\" in the original configuration.', 'trans_template': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/2 disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/1 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/2 disable\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/1 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" is to remove the \"disable\" statement from the interface configuration. The \"delete\" command is used to remove configuration statements in Juniper. I\\'ve included all three interface commands since they all had \"no shutdown\" in the original configuration.', 'trans_template': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/1 disable'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols router-advertisement interface all', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent command for `ipv6 enable`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols router-advertisement interface all\\n##\\n\\nNote: While Cisco's `ipv6 enable` simply activates IPv6 on an interface, Juniper requires a slightly different approach. The above command enables IPv6 router advertisements on all interfaces, which is the closest functional equivalent to activating IPv6 functionality.\\n\\nFor more precise interface-specific IPv6 activation in Juniper, you would typically assign an IPv6 address to the interface (which implicitly enables IPv6), like:\\n`set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:DB8:3::2/64`\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols router-advertisement interface all'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:DB8:1::1/64', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ipv6 address` command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:DB8:1::1/64\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- Juniper uses a hierarchical configuration structure\\n- `ge-1/0/2` is the Juniper equivalent of GigabitEthernet1/0/2\\n- `unit 0` is typically required on physical interfaces\\n- `family inet6` specifies IPv6 configuration\\n- The address format remains the same (2001:DB8:1::1/64)\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:DB8:1::1/64'}]]": 1,
